WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:08.870
My first master's topic spent years in cold storage. In 2019, I opened the freezer and discovered it was still asking for boundary conditions.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:20.790
The topic was aquifer thermal energy storage. I inherited the model, got stuck, and eventually learned that the original formulation had a small mathematical flaw. A very educational way to lose several months.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:32.854
Years later, a junior collaborator and I revived it. We corrected the model and placed a Robin-type boundary at the injection well, so temperature and heat flux could negotiate instead of pretending the well wall was perfect.

00:00:33.014 --> 00:00:43.286
Inside the aquifer, conduction smoothed the heat while dispersion stretched the thermal front. The paper compared five existing solutions, then tested when that well boundary actually mattered.

00:00:43.446 --> 00:00:53.694
Next came Morris global sensitivity analysis. Mathematica did not hand me the method, so I coded the screening algorithm myself. A global method, locally assembled, in about a week.

00:00:53.854 --> 00:01:02.638
At high Peclet numbers, ordinary precision became nervous. Arbitrary precision kept the solution standing. This is author recollection, not a theorem.

00:01:02.798 --> 00:01:15.110
The model then faced finite-difference results and a real A T E S experiment in Mobile, Alabama. The field forcing was not a clean rectangle. It was a badly behaved time series, like real operations usually are.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:23.910
The model reproduced the injection-period temperature reasonably well. But averaging the injection history could underestimate the early response. The paper said so.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:35.758
Then I looked for the full injection, storage, recovery cycle. Superposition declined the invitation. Injection and recovery do not share the same governing state, so the ending must become the next beginning.

00:01:35.918 --> 00:01:46.982
Reviewer Three's verdict? Accept the injection paper. Major revision for my younger self's belief that one analytical solution automatically becomes an operational tool. And yes, write the sequel.
